(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-cms-exam-1-master-exam-updated-form-b"]={"title":"CMS I Exam 1 Master Exam (Updated) — Form B","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 74-year-old man presents with a two-month history of intense itching followed by blistering. Examination shows numerous 1 to 3 cm tense, firm bullae on the trunk, flexural surfaces of the arms, and the axillary and inguinal folds. Rubbing the skin adjacent to a blister does not cause the epidermis to slough. There is a single small erosion in the mouth. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Dermatitis herpetiformis","Bullous pemphigoid","Pemphigus vulgaris","Bullous impetigo"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Bullous pemphigoid damages the epithelial basement membrane, producing subepithelial blistering, and classically presents after age 60 with tense 1 to 3 cm bullae on the trunk, extremity flexures, and axillary and inguinal folds. A pruritic prodrome may precede the bullae by weeks to months, and Nikolsky's sign is negative. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 123"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man presents with a lesion on the temple that he is unable to describe, and the clinician has not yet formed an impression. What is the recommended first step when evaluating any premalignant or malignant cutaneous lesion?","choices":["Characterise the lesion before naming it","Name the likely diagnosis, then seek support","Refer every pigmented lesion","Photograph and review next visit"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The foundational approach is to describe before naming: characterise each lesion systematically by primary lesion type, colour, surface texture, border definition, size, distribution, palpability, ulceration, and bleeding before assigning a diagnosis. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"A 68-year-old woman presents with chronic bilateral lower-leg swelling, pruritus, and skin discoloration. Examination demonstrates poorly demarcated erythematous plaques, pitting edema, and brown hyperpigmentation around both medial ankles. Pedal pulses are diminished. What is the most appropriate next step before prescribing compression therapy?","choices":["Order patch testing","Obtain a bacterial wound culture","Perform a skin biopsy","Perform an ankle-brachial index"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Compression is the cornerstone of management only once adequate arterial circulation has been established. An ankle-brachial index or toe pressure should be obtained when peripheral arterial disease is suspected — as the diminished pulses here suggest — before initiating substantial compression. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 116"},{"q":"A 63-year-old woman with obesity has a burning rash under both breasts. Examination shows well-demarcated beefy erythematous patches in the folds with peripheral scale and several small papules and pustules on adjacent skin just beyond the main area of redness. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Erythrasma","Contact dermatitis","Tinea corporis","Cutaneous candidiasis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Candidal intertrigo produces well-demarcated erythematous patches in warm moist folds with satellite papules and pustules beyond the main area, and pruritus with burning pain is typical. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man presents with vesicles in the right ophthalmic division distribution, including lesions on the tip of his nose. What does that finding signify?","choices":["Zoster sine herpete","Hutchinson sign, which increases ocular risk","Hutchinson sign, whose absence would exclude eye disease","Disseminated zoster"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Herpes zoster ophthalmicus involves the ophthalmic division of the trigeminal nerve. Hutchinson sign — lesions on the tip or side of the nose — increases ocular risk, but its absence does not exclude eye involvement, and systemic antiviral therapy should be started immediately. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 103"},{"q":"A 69-year-old man who has worked outdoors for forty years is examined. Several pink papules 0.3 to 0.5 cm are visible on the scalp, and running a hand across the skin reveals further lesions that could not be seen at all. What is the characteristic tactile finding?","choices":["Boggy and fluctuant","Sandpaper texture","Velvety texture","Pearly with telangiectasias"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Actinic keratoses are small 0.2 to 0.6 cm flesh-coloured, pink, or slightly hyperpigmented papules with a characteristic sandpaper texture on palpation, and a lesion may be more apparent by touch than by sight. The skin must therefore be palpated, not only inspected. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"A 62-year-old man has several soft whitish-yellow papules 3 to 5 mm on the forehead, each with a central dell. He is worried they are skin cancer. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Sebaceous hyperplasia","Basal cell carcinoma","Syringoma","Xanthelasma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Sebaceous hyperplasia is a common benign condition of enlarged sebaceous glands from changing sebocyte turnover with age, presenting as single or multiple whitish-yellow or skin-coloured soft papules 2 to 9 mm with a central dell. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 110"},{"q":"A 62-year-old man with thickened, discoloured toenails is having a specimen taken to confirm onychomycosis before oral therapy is started. From where should the specimen be taken?","choices":["The most proximal accessible diseased nail bed or subungual debris","The surrounding proximal nail fold","The distal free edge of the nail plate","A plucked hair from the affected limb"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Sampling should be from the most proximal accessible diseased nail bed and subungual debris, where viable organism is concentrated, using potassium hydroxide microscopy, periodic acid-Schiff stain of clippings, fungal culture, or polymerase chain reaction. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"A 3-week-old infant, born prematurely, has a bright red papule on the scalp that was a faint blanched area with fine telangiectasias at birth and has grown noticeably in the past fortnight. What growth pattern should the parents be told to expect?","choices":["No change during childhood","Rapid growth, then slow involution","Rapid growth with malignant risk","Steady growth with no involution"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Infantile hemangiomas have a unique growth pattern of rapid proliferation followed by slower involution, with rapid growth during the neonatal period and most growth in the first 4 to 6 months. The earliest sign is blanching of the involved skin followed by fine telangiectasias and then a red macule. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"A 45-year-old man has red, itchy, circular patches on the forearm, and the clinician is distinguishing tinea corporis from nummular eczema. What is the distinguishing feature?","choices":["Only tinea corporis is pruritic","Tinea corporis clears centrally; eczema does not","Only tinea corporis is well demarcated","Nummular eczema clears centrally; tinea does not"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Both present as red or pink itchy circular patches. Tinea corporis is a fungal infection with an annular appearance, whereas nummular eczema is non-infectious and coin-shaped without central clearing. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 36-year-old pregnant woman and her 7-year-old son both develop erythema migrans after a camping trip. What is first-line oral antibiotic therapy for Lyme disease, and what alternative applies to children and pregnant women with early disease?","choices":["Intravenous ceftriaxone for all stages","Doxycycline, or amoxicillin in pregnancy","Amoxicillin, with doxycycline for failure","No antibiotics for stage 1"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Antibiotics are indicated in all stages. Doxycycline is first line, with amoxicillin as an alternative first-line agent for early disease including erythema migrans, particularly in children and pregnant women. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"A student uses the VINDICATE mnemonic to build a differential for a 72-year-old man brought to the emergency department with confusion. What do the letters stand for?","choices":["Vascular, inflammatory, neurological, drug-related, idiopathic, cardiac, allergic, toxic, environmental","A checklist for appraising evidence","A physical examination sequence","Vascular, infectious, neoplastic, degenerative, iatrogenic, congenital, autoimmune, trauma, endocrine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. VINDICATE is a category-based framework: vascular, infectious, neoplastic, degenerative, iatrogenic, congenital, autoimmune, trauma, and endocrine or metabolic. Applied to confusion it yields stroke, meningitis, brain tumour, Alzheimer disease, and so on. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"A 26-year-old Black man reports tender bumps on his neck and jawline that appear after shaving. Examination shows erythematous papules, several with a visible central hair shaft curving back into the skin. What is the underlying process?","choices":["A foreign body reaction to re-entrant hair","Apocrine gland occlusion","An allergic reaction to shaving products","Follicular hyperkeratinisation with excess sebum"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Pseudofolliculitis barbae is a foreign body reaction to hair in any shaved area. The cut hair curves into the follicular wall and penetrates the skin to form a tender red papule or pustule, and it occurs commonly in Black men because of tightly curled facial hair together with keratin gene variations. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"A 76-year-old woman has had six weeks of severe itching followed by numerous tense, firm 2 cm blisters on the trunk, axillae, and inner thighs. Rubbing adjacent normal skin does not cause it to slough. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Dermatitis herpetiformis","Bullous pemphigoid","Erythema multiforme","Pemphigus vulgaris"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Bullous pemphigoid produces tense 1 to 3 cm bullae on the trunk, flexures, and intertriginous folds after age 60, often preceded by a pruritic prodrome of weeks to months, with a negative Nikolsky sign. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 123"},{"q":"A 62-year-old man with diabetes has three thickened, discoloured toenails with crumbling of the distal nail plate, subungual debris, and separation of the plate from the bed. He also has scaling between the toes. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Chronic paronychia","Nail psoriasis","Nail unit melanoma","Onychomycosis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Distal lateral onychomycosis produces debris, onycholysis, thickening, discoloration, and crumbling, and dermatophytes — especially Trichophyton rubrum — cause most cases. Coexisting tinea pedis is a recognised risk factor. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"A 26-year-old man has three smooth, round bald patches on the scalp that appeared over a month. The underlying scalp is smooth without scale, redness, or scarring, and short hairs narrow at the root are visible at the margins. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Lichen planopilaris","Trichotillomania","Alopecia areata","Androgenetic alopecia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Alopecia areata produces sudden round 1 to 4 cm patches of non-scarring hair loss with a smooth scalp, and exclamation point hairs at the margins are pathognomonic. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 138"},{"q":"A 25-year-old woman has a painful eruption on the vermilion border preceded by a day of tingling and burning. Examination shows grouped vesicles on an erythematous base, some broken down into shallow painful ulcers. She has had similar episodes before. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Aphthous ulceration","Herpes zoster","Herpes simplex virus","Impetigo"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Herpes simplex produces grouped vesicles on an erythematous base that break down into shallow painful ulcers, preceded by tenderness, paraesthesia, or burning, and it is characterised by latency with lifelong recurrence at the same site. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 119"},{"q":"A 59-year-old woman has a classic presentation of erysipelas with a sharply demarcated raised plaque of the shin and a fever of 39 degrees Celsius. What laboratory and imaging approach is appropriate?","choices":["Blood and tissue cultures in every patient","Magnetic resonance imaging for depth","Clinical; cultures and imaging add little","No laboratory testing is ever useful"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Erysipelas is a clinical diagnosis in a classic presentation. Leukocytosis and raised erythrocyte sedimentation rate and C-reactive protein are common, but blood and tissue cultures are not cost effective because of extremely low organism yield, and imaging studies are of low yield and not indicated. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 103"},{"q":"A 19-year-old returns from a tropical beach holiday with an intensely itchy, raised, serpentine track on the foot that has advanced a few centimetres over the past two days. What is the treatment?","choices":["Oral albendazole or ivermectin","Topical therapy alone","Excision of an embedded flea","Surgical excision or cryotherapy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Cutaneous larva migrans results from larvae of animal nematodes, mostly dog and cat hookworms, in which the human is a dead-end host. The classic lesion is an erythematous, raised, vesicular, linear or serpentine trail progressing 2 to 3 cm per day. Treatment is albendazole 400 mg daily for 3 days or ivermectin. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"A student proposes a rare diagnosis to explain a very typical presentation in a 30-year-old woman. What does the principle 'place your bets on uncommon manifestations of common conditions rather than common manifestations of uncommon conditions' mean in practice?","choices":["An atypical presentation of a common disease is likelier","Assume the most serious diagnosis first","All diagnoses are equally likely until tested","Rare diagnoses should never be considered"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Common things occur commonly — when you hear hoof beats think of horses, not zebras. An unusual presentation of a common condition is more probable than a textbook presentation of something rare. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 27-year-old woman develops a rash on the backs of her hands two days ago that has spread to the forearms and knees. Examination shows lesions with three concentric zones — a dusky centre, a pale ring, and an erythematous rim — fixed in place for four days. She had a cold sore two weeks ago. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Erythema multiforme","Fixed drug eruption","Pityriasis rosea","Urticaria"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Erythema multiforme produces characteristic fixed target lesions with three concentric zones in an acral distribution. Over 50 percent of cases are triggered by herpes simplex virus, which the recent cold sore supplies. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"A 29-year-old woman and her 31-year-old husband both have plaque psoriasis and ask about the likelihood their future children will be affected. What is the most accurate counselling?","choices":["About 41 percent of their children","About 50 percent; autosomal dominant","No increased risk; it is not heritable","About 8 percent of their children"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The figures given are 8 percent when one parent has psoriasis and 41 percent when both do, reflecting a genetic predisposition in which PSORS1 is the major susceptibility locus. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 155"},{"q":"A 6-week-old infant has a bright red papule on the shoulder, and the clinician classifies the lesion by the depth of vessel involvement. What are the three types of infantile hemangioma, and which is the most common?","choices":["Superficial, mixed, and deep; superficial commonest","Congenital, infantile, acquired","Superficial, mixed, and deep; deep commonest","Capillary, cavernous, arteriovenous"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The superficial type is the most common, with dilated vessels in the dermis surrounded by proliferating endothelial cells, presenting as a bright red papule, plaque, or nodule and previously called a strawberry hemangioma. The deep type is the least common. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"A 33-year-old man returns from a hotel stay with several itchy welts on exposed skin. Examination shows wheals and papules with a haemorrhagic punctum, arranged in rows of three. He did not feel the bites occur. What organism is responsible?","choices":["Tunga penetrans","Cimex lectularius","Sarcoptes scabiei","Schistosome cercariae"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Bedbugs are nocturnal feeders that hide in cracks and crevices of headboards, picture frames, and behind loose wallpaper. Bites are painless, multiple, and grouped in a linear fashion, and a row of three bites is known as breakfast, lunch, and dinner. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 6-year-old boy is brought in with fever and a rash. Examination shows non-blanching deposits of blood in the skin, several measuring 6 mm across. What is the most appropriate interpretation of this finding?","choices":["A wheal from dermal fluid","Purpura, an emergency until proven otherwise","Purpura, benign and observable","Petechiae, an emergency until proven otherwise"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Purpura is a deposit of blood 4 mm or greater, and the lecture flags it explicitly as a medical emergency until proven otherwise. Combined with fever in a child this demands immediate evaluation. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"A 22-year-old woman develops an intensely itchy eruption three days after gardening. Examination shows erythematous papules, vesicles, and a few bullae in linear streaks across both forearms, with lesions at differing stages. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Irritant contact dermatitis","Herpes zoster","Allergic contact dermatitis","Cutaneous larva migrans"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Urushiol sap from Toxicodendron species produces intensely pruritic vesicular lesions in linear distributions where the plant grazed the skin. Lesions erupt within 4 to 96 hours and may appear in multiple stages. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 87"},{"q":"An 82-year-old woman living alone reports generalised itching and rough, flaking skin each winter, worse on the lower legs. She takes long hot showers daily using a scented bar soap. Examination shows dry skin with fine scale and a few superficial fissures on the shins, without erythematous plaques or vesicles. What is the most appropriate advice?","choices":["A high-potency corticosteroid twice daily","A scented body wash daily","Short lukewarm showers; emollient within minutes","A keratolytic onto the fissures"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Xerosis reflects impaired stratum corneum hydration promoted by aging, low humidity, hot water, and detergents. Management is short lukewarm showers, a gentle fragrance-free cleanser used only where needed, and a thick ointment or cream applied within minutes of bathing. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 153"},{"q":"A 28-year-old man has a healing surgical wound and asks why the scar keeps getting stronger over months. What accounts for the improving tensile strength of a maturing scar?","choices":["Progressive cross-linking of collagen fibres","New elastin deposition","Vascular ingrowth","Strength is fixed at the time of closure"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The four phases are hemostasis, inflammation, proliferation, and remodeling. As the scar matures its tensile strength improves because of progressive cross-linking of collagen fibres. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman has a slow-growing 1 cm papule on the nose with a pearly translucent quality and visible telangiectasias that become more apparent when the skin is stretched. There is central erosion. What subtype is this?","choices":["Nodular basal cell carcinoma","Nodular squamous cell carcinoma","Keratoacanthoma","Amelanotic melanoma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Nodular basal cell carcinoma is a papule or nodule with central erosion, growing slowly over years to 1 to 2 cm or larger, with a pearly or translucent quality and visible telangiectasias accentuated by stretching the skin. Basal cell carcinoma is the most common form of cancer, driven by ultraviolet light in fair-skinned people with intense intermittent exposure. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman of Mediterranean descent presents with painful mouth erosions that began three months ago, followed by skin lesions. Examination shows flaccid bullae and widespread crusted erosions on the scalp, chest, axillae, and groin that bleed easily and are painful. Lateral pressure on normal-appearing skin causes the superficial layers to slip away. What does the biopsy most likely demonstrate?","choices":["Acantholysis with intraepithelial blisters","Granular immunoglobulin A at papillary tips","Band-like lymphocytes in the dermis","Neutrophils in a row at the junction"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Pemphigus is characterised by loss of keratinocyte-to-keratinocyte adhesion, termed acantholysis, producing intraepithelial blisters. The oral onset, flaccid easily ruptured bullae, and positive Nikolsky's sign all identify pemphigus, and biopsy demonstrating acantholysis is the diagnostic finding. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 132"},{"q":"A 32-year-old woman with recurrent genital herpes simplex virus type 2 infection is in a relationship with an uninfected partner. Which treatment approach also reduces transmission risk?","choices":["Daily suppressive valacyclovir","Prophylaxis for the uninfected partner","Patient-initiated episodic therapy","No antiviral therapy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Recurrent genital herpes may be managed with patient-initiated episodic therapy or daily suppressive therapy, and suppressive valacyclovir lowers herpes simplex virus type 2 transmission. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 124"},{"q":"A 29-year-old Black man with pseudofolliculitis barbae cannot stop shaving because his employer requires a clean-shaven face. What advice about shaving technique is most appropriate?","choices":["Use a multi-blade lift-and-cut razor","Shave daily against the grain","Avoid lift-and-cut razors; use a single blade","Keep shaving; rely on a topical steroid"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Patients should avoid lift-and-cut razor systems and look for mild razor angles with single or at most double blades, because those systems cut the hair below the skin surface where it can re-enter the follicular wall. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"A 6-year-old has fifteen discrete, smooth, firm, flesh-coloured dome-shaped papules averaging 4 mm on the trunk and arms, each with a central dimple. His parents ask about treatment. What is a reasonable approach?","choices":["Observation; procedures may scar or pigment","Oral acyclovir","Salicylic acid 40 percent to each lesion","Curettage of every lesion at the first visit"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Molluscum contagiosum is a benign poxvirus infection, and observation is appropriate for many patients because procedures may blister, pigment, or scar. Approved options such as berdazimer gel and cantharidin exist when treatment is chosen. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 136"},{"q":"A 36-year-old man has had hidradenitis suppurativa of both axillae for eight years despite antibiotics and repeated drainage, and asks whether anything can cure it. What offers the best chance of permanent cure?","choices":["Intralesional triamcinolone acetonide injections","Incision and drainage of each cyst as it develops","Topical clindamycin used indefinitely","Wide excision of the affected areas"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Chances of permanent cure are best with wide excision of the affected areas. Large fluctuant cysts are incised and drained, but that manages an episode rather than curing the disease. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"A 38-year-old man with a family history of melanoma has more than 100 naevi, several at least 5 mm with irregular, indistinct borders. How is a dysplastic naevus diagnosed, and what is the management?","choices":["Biopsy, excising where melanoma is a concern","Clinical diagnosis; no biopsy needed","No follow-up once documented","Biopsy, then excise all naevi prophylactically"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Diagnosis of a dysplastic melanocytic nevus is by biopsy. Management includes observation, biopsy of all changing or developing lesions, excision where there is concern for melanoma, and sun protection. Family history is a risk factor, and dysplastic nevus syndrome may produce over 100 naevi by adolescence. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman has itchy, discoloured skin over both lower legs. Examination shows poorly demarcated erythematous and brown-pigmented patches in the gaiter region of both legs, with pitting oedema and varicosities. She is afebrile and both legs are affected equally. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Stasis dermatitis","Nummular eczema","Contact dermatitis","Cellulitis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Stasis dermatitis follows chronic venous hypertension and produces pruritic erythematous, violaceous, or hyperpigmented patches in the gaiter region of one or both legs, with oedema and brown haemosiderin staining. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 114"},{"q":"A 16-year-old football player develops acne confined to the shoulders and upper back under his pads. What predisposing factor does this represent?","choices":["Genetic predisposition","Acne mechanica from pressure on the follicle","Endocrine acne from insulin resistance","Drug-induced acne from an anabolic steroid"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Acne mechanica results from pressure on the skin — shoulder pads, orthopaedic casts, and helmets are the named examples — occluding the pilosebaceous follicle. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"A 27-year-old pregnant woman has several lesions on the upper trunk consisting of a central arteriole with radiating capillaries. What is the mechanism, and what should be asked in the history?","choices":["Dilated dermal capillaries present from birth","Dilated existing vessels; ask about oestrogen and alcohol","Capillary proliferation with age; no history needed","Blood extravasated into the dermis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Nevus araneus involves no vascular proliferation and is due to dilation of pre-existing vessels. Oestrogen excess states may be the cause, and lesions resolve after delivery or cessation of hormonal contraception. History should cover pregnancies, hormone use, alcohol, and high-risk medications for liver damage. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 89"},{"q":"An 8-year-old boy has three smooth, non-scarring patches of hair loss on the scalp with exclamation point hairs at the margins and fine pitting of several fingernails. A potassium hydroxide preparation of plucked hairs is negative. What is the most appropriate first-line treatment?","choices":["Oral prednisone","Topical corticosteroids","Oral terbinafine","Intralesional corticosteroids"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Topical steroids are first line in children 10 years old and younger. The child is 8, which places him in that group and avoids the discomfort of intralesional injection in a young child. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 140"},{"q":"A 51-year-old woman with known rosacea reports a gritty foreign-body sensation, blurred vision, and photophobia. What is the most appropriate action?","choices":["Reassure the patient that ocular symptoms are unrelated to rosacea","Urgent ophthalmology referral","Begin topical metronidazole and reassess in six weeks","Routine ophthalmology referral within three months"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Ocular rosacea with keratitis, visual symptoms, or corneal involvement warrants urgent ophthalmology referral. Visual symptoms and photophobia raise exactly those concerns. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"A 54-year-old woman with obesity has multiple soft, skin-coloured pedunculated papules 2 to 6 mm on the neck and in both axillae. They are asymptomatic apart from occasional catching on clothing. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Verruca vulgaris","Acrochordon","Neurofibroma","Seborrheic keratosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Acrochordons are fibroepithelial pedunculated papillomas with a narrow stalk and broad tip, occurring in areas of friction such as the neck, axilla, and groin, with increased incidence in women and in obesity. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"A 26-year-old woman has painful red lumps on both shins for ten days. Examination shows bilateral tender erythematous nodules 2 to 4 cm on the anterior tibial surfaces. None has ulcerated, and several are fading to a bruise-like colour. She had a sore throat a month ago. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Pyoderma gangrenosum","Insect bite reactions","Erythema nodosum","Nodular vasculitis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Erythema nodosum is a septal panniculitis producing bilateral tender non-ulcerating nodules 1 to 5 cm on the anterior shins, evolving over 3 to 6 weeks through bruise-like colours. Group A streptococcus is the commonest infectious trigger. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"A 38-year-old farmer has tender, boggy, pustular plaques in the beard area. Hairs in the affected area are easily removed. What therapy is required?","choices":["Oral cephalexin","A change of shaving technique","Oral antifungal therapy","A topical antifungal cream alone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Hair is easily removed in tinea barbae unlike in bacterial folliculitis, and a bacterial culture can be done to rule the latter out. Topical antifungals do not penetrate the hair follicle, so oral therapy with griseofulvin or terbinafine is required. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man with diabetes has an asymptomatic brownish patch in the crural region and scaling between the fourth and fifth toes. Under a Wood's lamp the affected skin fluoresces coral-red. What is first-line treatment for localised disease?","choices":["Topical erythromycin","Topical miconazole","Topical terbinafine","Topical mupirocin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Erythrasma is a chronic superficial infection of intertriginous skin caused by Corynebacterium minutissimum invading the upper third of the stratum corneum. Coral-red fluorescence under a Wood's lamp is diagnostic, and first-line treatment for localised disease is topical erythromycin or clindamycin. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"A 40-year-old woman has had urticarial lesions for two months. Individual lesions persist for more than 24 hours and resolve leaving brownish discoloration. She reports arthralgia. What should be suspected?","choices":["Urticarial vasculitis","Ordinary chronic urticaria","Hereditary angioedema","Anaphylaxis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Urticarial vasculitis is distinguished by lesions persisting beyond 24 hours that leave bruising, with low complement levels and a biopsy showing leukocytoclastic vasculitis. Ordinary wheals blanch fully and last under 24 hours. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"A 28-year-old woman has an annular eruption of the forearms and the clinician is distinguishing erythema multiforme from urticaria. What feature most reliably separates them?","choices":["Only erythema multiforme itches","Wheals stay fixed for several days","Only erythema multiforme blanches","Wheals migrate and last under 24 hours"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The differential states that urticarial wheals are pruritic, migratory, and blanch fully, with no fixed target lesions and individual lesions lasting less than 24 hours. Fixity over days is what identifies erythema multiforme. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"A 46-year-old woman reports gradual hair thinning over several years. Examination shows diffuse thinning across the central and parietal scalp with preservation of the frontal hairline. There are no bald patches, no scaling, no scarring, and no exclamation point hairs. Several of her male relatives began balding in their twenties. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Alopecia areata","Lichen planopilaris","Male-pattern hair loss","Female-pattern hair loss"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Female-pattern hair loss produces diffuse thinning of the central and parietal scalp without significant change to the frontal hairline. There is an increased frequency of balding among first-degree male relatives, which the family history here supplies. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 146"},{"q":"A 38-year-old man presents with thick scaly plaques on both elbows and knees that have been present for years. Examination shows well-demarcated salmon-pink plaques with adherent silvery scale over the extensor elbows and knees, the sacrum, and the scalp. A linear plaque has developed along a surgical scar on his forearm. When scale is lifted from one plaque, pinpoint bleeding appears. What is this bleeding phenomenon called?","choices":["Auspitz sign","Wickham striae","Nikolsky sign","Koebner phenomenon"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Auspitz sign is the appearance of bleeding when scale is removed from a psoriatic plaque, produced by the dilated capillaries lying immediately beneath a thinned suprapapillary epidermis. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 159"},{"q":"A 69-year-old man has a dermatomal vesicular eruption and a student asks where zoster most often appears. Which dermatomes are most frequently involved?","choices":["Thoracic about 55 percent, cranial about 20 percent","Lumbar in the majority","Cranial about 55 percent, thoracic about 20 percent","No dermatome predominates"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Thoracic dermatomes are most frequently involved at about 55 percent, cranial at about 20 percent, with lumbar and sacral distributions following. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 100"},{"q":"A 9-year-old boy has head lice that persisted after a first pediculicide application. Why is a multimodal approach recommended, and what does the World Health Organization recommend regarding pediculicide assessment?","choices":["Head lice are self-limiting","Resistance is rising; read the result at 24 hours","Resistance is falling; read it immediately","Essential oils are first line"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A multimodal approach is warranted because of increasing resistance, and the World Health Organization recommends that pediculicidal testing be read 24 hours after application. If treatment is not followed appropriately, patients tend to seek alternative care such as essential oils. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"A 14-year-old boy with neurofibromatosis type 1 has a large, diffuse soft tissue mass running along a nerve in the thigh. What is a plexiform neurofibroma, and what is the concern with it?","choices":["A discrete nerve sheath tumour at the surface","A pigmented macule growing with the child","A large tumour in nerve covering that may invade","A tumour confined to brain and spinal cord"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Plexiform neurofibromas are tumours in the tissue covering nerves, occurring anywhere except the brain and spinal cord. They are large and extensive and may be locally invasive, so clinical evaluation is needed. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 101"},{"q":"A 30-year-old man has confirmed tungiasis of both feet after a trip abroad. What management is appropriate?","choices":["Excision or cryotherapy, with tetanus cover","Professional extermination of the home","Antihistamines and oatmeal baths alone","Oral albendazole for 3 days"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Management includes surgical excision or cryotherapy and topical agents, tetanus prophylaxis, and systemic antibiotics. Prevention centres on avoiding walking barefoot in endemic areas. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 4-month-old has a large facial hemangioma that is beginning to obstruct the visual axis of one eye. What is first-line treatment?","choices":["Serial observation alone","Immediate surgical excision in all cases","Pulsed dye laser as the primary treatment","An oral beta-blocker such as propranolol"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Beta-blockers are first-line treatment, for example oral propranolol or timolol. Indications for treatment include cosmetic concern, functional involvement such as blocked vision, deep ulceration, and infection. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"A 24-year-old woman presents with an itchy rash on her forearm. She had a laceration dressed with an adhesive bandage four days ago. Examination shows a sharply demarcated rectangular erythematous plaque with tiny vesicles corresponding exactly to the adhesive portions of the dressing, and a rectangle of normal skin in the centre where the cotton pad had lain. The laceration itself is clean and healing. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Herpes simplex virus reactivation","Allergic contact dermatitis to the adhesive","Wound infection of the underlying laceration","Cellulitis of the forearm"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Patients allergic to the adhesive in an adhesive bandage present with a bandage-shaped rash that spares the middle area where the cotton lay. The eruption reproduces the shape of the contactant, which is the signature of allergic contact dermatitis. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 86"},{"q":"A 62-year-old woman has several uniformly tan-brown macules on the dorsal hands and face that have appeared gradually over years. One lesion is larger, asymmetric, and has irregular borders with darker areas within it. What should be done about that lesion?","choices":["Treat it as a seborrheic keratosis and leave it alone","Evaluate it with dermoscopy for lentigo maligna","Treat it as a café-au-lait macule and observe","Reassure her that it is a solar lentigo like the others"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Lentigo maligna, which is melanoma in situ, is asymmetric with irregular border and colour and darker areas, and dermoscopy is described as essential. A solar lentigo that has diverged from its uniform neighbours requires that evaluation. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 111"},{"q":"A 61-year-old man has had erythematous scaly patches and plaques on the trunk for four years, several larger than 5 cm, that have been treated as psoriasis and eczema without lasting response. Pruritus is prominent. What features discriminate this from the inflammatory conditions it resembles?","choices":["Pruritus","A positive potassium hydroxide preparation","Chronicity, treatment resistance, odd sites","Truncal involvement"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Mycosis fungoides may resemble psoriasis, eczema, or tinea, and the discriminators are chronicity, treatment resistance, and large or oddly distributed lesions. Early morphology is localised or generalised erythematous patches or scaly plaques, usually on the trunk, frequently greater than 5 cm. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man has multiple light to dark brown macules with well-defined but irregular borders that tend to coalesce over his shoulders, where he has had severe sunburns. What is the underlying process and its epidemiological association?","choices":["Basal melanocyte proliferation with more melanin","T cell destruction of melanocytes","Dermal capillary proliferation","Somatic mutation present from birth"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Solar lentigines arise from proliferation of basal melanocytes with increased melanin production and are strongly associated with older age, present in about 90 percent by age 50, along with sun damage, tanning, a history of ephelides, and birth control use. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 74-year-old man still has burning, stabbing pain four months after a thoracic zoster eruption cleared. What is first-line treatment?","choices":["Long-term opioids","Acetaminophen alone","Antivirals","Gabapentin or a tricyclic antidepressant"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Pain persisting 90 days or more after rash onset is the commonly used definition. First-line treatment is gabapentin or pregabalin, an appropriate tricyclic antidepressant, or topical lidocaine, with a capsaicin patch as a further option. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 109"},{"q":"A 29-year-old woman has burning and small bumps around her mouth. Examination shows grouped monomorphic erythematous papules and papulopustules on the chin and nasolabial folds, with a narrow band of normal skin next to the lip margin. There are no comedones. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Impetigo","Perioral dermatitis","Acne rosacea","Seborrheic dermatitis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Perioral dermatitis produces grouped monomorphic papules, papulovesicles, or papulopustules around the mouth, nose, or eyes, with a narrow zone beside the vermilion border characteristically spared. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 99"},{"q":"A 45-year-old fair-skinned woman reports facial flushing that worsens with sun exposure, hot drinks, and alcohol. Examination shows persistent central facial erythema with telangiectasias across the cheeks and nose, and she describes her skin as stinging easily. There are no papules, no pustules, and no comedones. What subtype does she have?","choices":["Phymatous rosacea","Papulopustular rosacea","Erythematotelangiectatic rosacea","Acne vulgaris"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The erythematotelangiectatic subtype produces persistent central facial erythema, flushing, and telangiectasias with sensitive stinging skin, and specifically no papules or pustules. It is the most common subtype. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"A 45-year-old man has a small area of cellulitis of the forearm with minimal pain, no systemic signs, and no comorbidity. In which patient with cellulitis is no diagnostic workup required?","choices":["Any patient already on antibiotics","Limited area, no systemic signs","Any patient who is afebrile","Any cellulitis of the lower leg"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Cellulitis is usually a clinical diagnosis, and no workup is needed when there is a limited area of involvement, minimal pain, no systemic signs, and no risk factor for serious illness such as extremes of age or immunocompromise. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 109"},{"q":"A 6-month-old girl is brought to the clinic by her father for a rash that has been present for three weeks. He reports she is difficult to settle at night and rubs her face against the bedsheet rather than scratching it. Her father has allergic rhinitis and her older brother has asthma. On examination there are weeping inflammatory patches and crusted plaques over both cheeks and the extensor surfaces of the forearms. The antecubital and popliteal fossae are clear. There is no fever, no purulent drainage, and no history of new detergents, soaps, or foods. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Atopic dermatitis, infantile pattern","Seborrheic dermatitis","Allergic contact dermatitis","Atopic dermatitis, childhood pattern"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In infants atopic dermatitis produces weeping inflammatory patches and crusted plaques on the cheeks, scalp, and extensor surfaces, and rubbing commonly replaces scratching at this age. The family history of allergic rhinitis and asthma completes the atopic triad, and roughly 80 percent of patients have a personal or family history of atopy. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"A 29-year-old woman presents with painful red lumps on both shins that appeared ten days ago. Examination shows bilateral tender erythematous nodules 1 to 5 cm on the anterior tibial surfaces. None has ulcerated, and several are fading to a bruise-like colour. She had a sore throat three weeks ago. What is the key distinguishing feature of this condition?","choices":["The nodules do not ulcerate","The nodules followed a streptococcal infection","The nodules are located on the anterior shins","The nodules are bilateral"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. That the nodules do not ulcerate is named as the key distinguishing feature. Erythema nodosum is a septal panniculitis without vasculitis, evolving over 3 to 6 weeks from bright red through the bruise-like colours the stem describes. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"A 73-year-old man has multiple actinic keratoses scattered across a bald scalp, and the clinician explains that treating only the individual visible lesions may be insufficient. What concept does this reflect?","choices":["Photoallergy","Koebner phenomenon","Field cancerisation","Pathergy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Actinic keratoses represent a field cancerisation process, meaning the ultraviolet-induced mutations extend through the surrounding skin rather than being confined to the visible lesions. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 112"},{"q":"A 7-year-old boy is seen for a recurrent, intensely pruritic rash. Examination shows poorly demarcated erythematous plaques with excoriations and lichenification of the antecubital and popliteal fossae. His mother reports a history of asthma. There is no fever, drainage, crusting, or tenderness. What is the most appropriate initial treatment?","choices":["Systemic prednisone","Emollients alone, without a steroid","Topical steroid plus emollient","Oral cephalexin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Treatment combines barrier repair with inflammation control: low to medium potency topical corticosteroid for the body, applied sparingly, with an emollient applied after rinsing. Neither element alone is adequate, which is what makes this the complete answer. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 57"}]};
